## Addendum 2026-08-31 (Jer peer round, batch 1) — operational kill class from the account layer

- **E-22 | account_spend_limit_batch_kill** — the provider ACCOUNT's spend limit (monthly
  cap / weekly window) exhausts MID-BATCH and kills every in-flight subagent with an HTTP
  429 rate-limit error within minutes. Seen: 1 batch / 6 agents (Jer peer batch 1,
  2026-08-28: all six peer a1 attempts killed; three at the deliverable-write step, one a
  just-resumed E-14 survivor; ZERO partial writes — every deliverable path verified
  ABSENT). Distinct from E-14 (transport) and E-13 (content filter): the kill is
  DETERMINISTIC until the account window resets or the owner raises the limit — an
  immediate retry is a guaranteed-futile blind retry, banned. Root cause: account-level
  quota shared across sessions, invisible to the orchestrator until the first 429.
  Detector: the failure notice's rate_limit/429 + spend-limit message naming the reset
  time. Cure: STOP launching immediately; record batch state + reset time; on window
  reset (or an owner limit-raise), re-verify governance, then apply the E-14 deliverable
  ladder per agent (ABSENT -> resume-in-place SAME attempt id — transcript context
  survives this kill class, preserving pre-kill byte work; PRESENT -> validate, never
  re-run). Pre-kill token spend is real but the kill notices carry no usage blocks —
  record the accounting gap honestly in the census. RE-SCAN: n/a (operational; no
  artifact persists). Machine-checkable: PARTIAL (the 429/spend-limit message class).

