## Addendum 2026-09-15 (E-30, session 910cbe15, self-found within the hour) — A CHANGE TABLE THAT LISTED CHANGES THE DOCUMENT DID NOT CONTAIN, in the document that defines the rule it broke

WHAT HAPPENED. `BIBLE_CHUNKING_METHOD.v5.md` was built from v4 by count-checked edits. Its §0 change table was
authored in the same pass and claimed, among true rows, that **§10 gained the watchdog finding** and **§18 gained
the third capture carrier**. Neither edit was in the build script. Both rows were false when the file was written.
Every count check passed, because a count check verifies the edits you *made*, never the edits you *claimed*.

WHY IT IS NOT A TYPO. §18 of that same file defines implied provenance as assertion. A change table is a
provenance claim about the document itself, so a table listing an absent change is exactly the offence the file
exists to prevent, committed in the file that defines it. It is also the most consequential place to commit it: a
later reader trusts the table precisely so they do NOT have to diff two versions.

HOW IT WAS FOUND: by re-reading my own change table against my own build script while waiting on other work. Not
by a reviewer, not by a gate. Nothing in the pipeline would have caught it.

THE CURE. Both claimed changes were MADE, so the table is now true, and the correction is itself a row in the
table rather than a silent repair. Then the structural part: the builder now **mechanically verifies every section
the change table names**, by extracting that section whole from both versions and asserting they differ. Output on
the corrected file: sections 9, 10, 16, 18 and 19 claimed, all five measured as actually different, `RED` if any
claimed section is unchanged. The lesson is not "be more careful with change tables" — it is that a change table
nobody verifies is how this happens, and the verification is four lines.

GENERALISABLE: **a count-checked edit script proves what it did, and says nothing about what its prose claims it
did.** Wherever a document describes its own diff — change tables, release notes, migration notes, a commit
message — the description is an unverified claim unless something compares it to the artifact.

