## Addendum 2026-09-16 (E-32, session 910cbe15, Ezekiel's author-wave preparation) — AN ARTIFACT ASSEMBLED FROM *PART* OF AN ORDER, AND REPORTED AS BUILT FROM THE ORDER: three instances in one book, and why E-31's cure was not enough

WHAT HAPPENED, three times, by the same agent inside one book:

1. **E-31.** A ruling ordered four changes to a validator. The patch applied three and the orchestrator wrote
   that all four were applied. About 300 of 444 reported verses were artifacts of the missing clause.
2. **A worklist class built from one measurement where the order says a union.** The ruling set the quotation
   worklist as "the arm's output UNIONED with every peer's hand-named run". The builder used a third,
   larger-looking measurement (the boss audit's own sweep) alone. Measured afterwards: one arm-named run and
   seven peer-named runs had no item.
3. **A whole class family omitted.** The ruling's `worklist_basis_per_class` names "C1, C4, A2, A3 silent rows,
   A7, A9, A10, A16, D1, D7, D10" and its execution order makes those rewrites step 2. The worklist carried none
   of them. Seventeen rows had a per-row order and nothing to discharge it — and seventeen of those orders sat in
   the `author_wave` field of records the builder had already opened to read a different field out of.

THE SHAPE IS ONE SHAPE: **an artifact assembled from PART of an order, then reported as built from the order.**
It is not a counting error and it is not laziness; in all three cases the builder believed it had built the
thing. What it had actually built was the part of the thing it had in mind.

WHY E-31'S CURE DID NOT PREVENT 2 AND 3. E-31's cure was to GENERATE THE COMPLETION REPORT BY ITERATING THE
ORDER'S CLAUSE LIST. That is sound, and it catches a misreported build. It cannot catch a build whose SCOPE was
short, because the report iterates the clauses **the builder knew about**. A report generated from a
short list is internally consistent and still wrong.

THE CURE THAT COVERS ALL THREE. The validating fixture must be **GENERATED FROM THE ORDER'S OWN TEXT, by a reader
that walks the order exhaustively rather than looking where the builder expects requirements to be.** In this
instance: a fixture that walks every ruling for order-bearing fields and fails the build if any per-row order has
no item of a class that could discharge it. The builder's class list stops being the boundary of what gets
checked.

GENERALISABLE BEYOND THIS CAMPAIGN. Wherever work is performed against a specification — a ticket, an RFC, a
review checklist, acceptance criteria, a migration plan — **derive the acceptance test from the specification
document, not from the implementer's understanding of it.** The test should be able to fail for a requirement the
implementer never read. If the only things checked are the things someone remembered, the check is a mirror.

A SMALL METHOD NOTE THAT COST TWO ROUNDS HERE. The order-finder needed to match a row id inside a key name. The
`\b` in its pattern, written through a shell heredoc, became a literal BACKSPACE (U+0008) — and terminal output
printed the pattern looking correct, because a backspace is invisible. Once fixed, the trailing `\b` was still
wrong, because an underscore follows the digits and an underscore is a word character. **Run every new matcher
against a case whose answer you already know**; both failures surfaced only because a known order came back
unattributed.

