## E-63 (2026-09-24) - RECEIPTS GAINED A FIELD AND A ROW KIND THAT NO READER KNEW, AND THE PIN GUARD WOULD HAVE LANDED A RUNNING EXECUTION ON AN AMENDMENT

**What happened.** The attempt receipts are append-only. During Ezekiel they came to hold two things their readers were
not written for:
- amending rows, which name an earlier execution by `amends_execution_id`;
- two token fields, `tokens_notification_unit` and `tokens_spend_unit`. I wrote these on five Ezekiel landing rows (the
  v9 author, v9 lanes a and b, and v10 lanes a and b) and taught no reader to read them.

Every reader kept its own reading of the receipts, and three went wrong. Their figures below were MEASURED when the cure
was applied and are TRANSCRIBED here from `sp_durable/Dan/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md` (entry "the amend fold reaches every
receipt reader").
1. **Metrics and the OW-12 status tool.** They missed the unit fields. They also counted each amending row as an
   execution, and added spend-unit amendment tokens to a notification-unit total. The Ezekiel figure given to the owner
   in the OW-12 close update was 71,188,346 tokens over 278 executions. Folded, it is 70,428,450 over 231. The difference
   has three parts:
   - 47 amending rows are no longer counted as executions;
   - -1,431,971 spend-unit tokens come out of a notification-unit figure;
   - +672,075 notification-unit tokens come in from the unit fields.
   Daniel had 3 executions, not 4.
2. **The capture index.** It did not fold at all.
   - Every amending row became a phantom run: 7 in Ezekiel and 1 in Daniel.
   - Four Ezekiel job pairs had their runs swapped: `ezek_pr_LF_c21`, `LF_c22`, `OL_c20` and `OL_c21`. Each #e1
     watchdog-failure row had been back-filled into the file after its #e2 landing row, and the index derived the run
     number from row order, so it indexed the landed run as #e1.
3. **The in-flight pin guard.** It took any non-launch row that names an execution as that execution's landing. I found
   this while deciding whether to amend three Daniel launch rows whose `parent_agent_id` is None. An amendment of a
   running execution would have landed it and released every pin it holds. I did not amend, and cured the guard first.

**Severity, scored on counterfactual blast radius.** Observed impact: medium. Wrong execution and token figures reached
the owner in an OW-12 update, and the owner uses those figures to plan. No pin was released, and no file changed under a
running lane. Counterfactual: high.
- One routine amendment of a running lane would have unpinned its inputs. That opens the E-41 class, a file regenerated
  under an agent that is reading it, with nothing to show that it happened.
- The OWED OW-26 transcript audit reads the capture index to find each job's landed run. For four jobs it would have
  audited the failed run's transcript as the landed one.

**Root cause.** The row vocabulary grew in the writer and nowhere else. Each reader carried a private, unstated idea of
which rows and fields exist, and nothing refused a row it did not understand. The guard's landing rule dates from when
every non-launch row was a landing; the amending rows made that false without touching the guard. I added the fields
and the row kind without first finding every reader.

**Cure (durable).** Digests are MEASURED.
